  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAn active pattern setting up across the region as the upper low\nthat has brought severe weather to AZ has moved onshore into CA\nand the southwest flow over the Borderland continues to increase.\nDew points currently up into the 50s for much of the CWA and\ninstability/shear increasing. CAPE values near AZ border\napproaching 1000J/kg and combined with 35-45KTS shear, severe\nstorms are possible. Also, KLSB observation currently 13013G22KTS\nwould add to some increased 0-1km low level shear/helicity. Could\nsee a tornado develop with initial storms which will likely occur\nby 20Z. Tornado watch will be out until early evening. Looking\nlike heavy rain threat with training possibilities over the Gila\nregion and possibly down into northern Hidalgo, so will leave\nFlood Watch as is. Rainfall amounts of 0.5-1.0\" with localized\n2-3\" possible.  \n\nAs we go into Fri-Sat, upper low becomes established over AZ and\nwill be slow to move out. There will be a shift in higher PoPs\ntoward the RGV for Fri and then eastern areas for Sat. Increasing\nmid level flow with a SSE surface wind will continue to keep shear\nvalues high and instability will be there as well, so severe\nthreat continues. Not sure the tornado threat will be as high as\ntoday though. Again, locally heavy rain will be possible with the\nabove normal PW's and training threat there. NBM PoPs seemed\nreasonable and higher than the operational GFS, but more in line\nwith the GEFS which puts high chance to low likely PoPs to the\nRGV. May need to put a Flood Watch out later for at least \nHudspeth and Otero counties.\n\nSunday is the questionable day. Model trend is to slow down the\ntiming of the upper low moving out. The trough axis will at least\nremain off to our west through 00Z Mon and with that, will keep\nsome PoPs going east of the Rio Grande Valley. Best moisture will\nbe moving out, but still some convective potential for moderate\nrainfall and a marginal severe threat over far eastern zones.  \n\nStill keeping a slight trough through the east even into Monday\nwith some rain chances continuing and even a continued low severe\nthreat. Tuesday looks drier with ridge passing overhead but rain\nchances return Wed. Temperatures by Sunday will be getting closer\nto normal and remain there.",
